🪜 Step 1: Set sensor and CFE formats
sudo media-ctl -d /dev/media3 --set-v4l2 '"imx219 17-0010":0[fmt:SRGGB10_1X10/1920x1080]'
sudo media-ctl -d /dev/media3 --set-v4l2 '"csi2":1[fmt:SRGGB16_1X16/1920x1080]'
sudo media-ctl -d /dev/media3 --set-v4l2 '"pisp-fe":0[fmt:SRGGB16_1X16/1920x1080]'
sudo media-ctl -d /dev/media3 --set-v4l2 '"rp1-cfe-fe-image0":0[fmt:SRGGB16_1X16/1920x1080]'

🪜 Step 2: Configure BE input and link
sudo media-ctl -d /dev/media0 --set-v4l2 '"pispbe-input":0[fmt:YUYV8_2X8/1920x1080]'
sudo media-ctl -l '"rp1-cfe-fe-image0":0 -> "pispbe-input":0[1]'


✅ This command creates a live link from your CFE output (/dev/video4) to the BE input (/dev/video20).

🪜 Step 3: Stream from BE output

Now stream and dump to file:

v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video23 --set-fmt-video=width=1920,height=1080,pixelformat=YUYV --stream-mmap --stream-count=10 --stream-to=/tmp/test.yuv


Then check:

ls -lh /tmp/test.yuv


You should now see a non-zero file size (≈4 MB per 1920×1080 YUYV frame × number of frames).

If it still shows 0 bytes, we’ll check:

v4l2-ctl --device=/dev/video4 --stream-mmap --stream-count=5
